WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.470
Hello there!

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.970
Today we are going to talk about positional
embeddings.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:19.970
You should have heard of them, because
wherever you see a transformer neural network,

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:23.850
you also see positional embeddings attached
to them.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:29.400
And because transformers are now literally
everywhere in machine learning, irrespectively

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.720
of the data type, you should have come across
positional embeddings already.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.320
But what are positional embeddings, why do
we need them in transformers in the first

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.340
place, and how do they work?

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:49.479
Well, today we are going to lift the mystery
over positional embeddings.

00:00:49.479 --> 00:00:54.220
Transformers are more and more important for
machine learning and have been proven to work

00:00:54.220 --> 00:01:01.180
very well on any kind of data, especially
when there is a lot of it to pretrain through

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.180
self-supervision.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.960
A big part of the data that we are interested
in,

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.170
comes in a specific order.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:13.340
If we change this order,
the meaning of the input might also change.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:19.450
The transformer does not process the input
in order, sequentially, but in parallel.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.941
For each element, it combines information
from the other elements through self-attention,

00:01:23.941 --> 00:01:28.730
but each element does this aggregation on
its own,

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:34.500
independently of what the other elements do
or have done yet.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:40.400
Because the transformer architecture per se,
does not model the order of the input anywhere,

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.910
we must encode the order of the input explicitly,
for the transformer to know that one piece

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.920
comes after the other and not in any other
permutation.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:57.200
This is where positional embeddings come in:
They are sort of an identifier, an obvious

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:04.950
hint for the transformer encoding the whereabouts
of a piece of the input within the sequence.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:08.649
These embeddings are
then added to the initial vector representation

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:10.129
of the input.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:13.700
This addition can be intuitively understood
as:

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:19.560
look! the initial vector representation of
the word “queen” is here in the multidimensional

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.810
space.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.510
It is close to “king”, because of word
frequency distribution reasons -- king and

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.170
queen often occur in the same context.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:34.710
But then, we add this positional embedding
identifying the order of the word.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:40.720
Hereby we say: “queen” should move a bit
along this dimension towards this specific

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:48.120
part of the space to cluster with all the
other first tokens in any other sequence.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.709
Space and dimension which we do not really
have in this 2d drawing, but you get the idea

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:59.690
that by adding these vectors we move the initial
representations into the direction of the

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.930
other first tokens, or the second tokens,
and so on.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:07.850
At least, this is how Ms. Coffee Bean pictures
it and you will possibly ask:

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:13.250
But how can this little multi-dimensional
shifting help the transformer?

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:19.510
Well, remember we are working with a multi-dimensional
spurious correlation identifying beast

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:24.140
which is a neural network,
therefore why should these systematic shifts

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.760
not be enough of a hint for it?

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:34.980
So, in short, positional embeddings are order
or position identifiers added to vectors for

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:38.390
the transformer to know the order of the sequence.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:41.930
These positional embeddings, must fulfil some
requirements:

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:48.570
First, every position should have the same
identifier, irrespectively of the sequence

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:52.010
length or of what exactly the input is.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:57.680
So while the sequence might change, the positional
embedding stays the same.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:03.910
Secondly, because the embeddings push the
original vectors by a bit into let’s say…

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:08.670
the “first token club”,
they should not be too large,

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:14.760
otherwise they push the vectors into very,
very distinct subspaces where the positional

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.709
similarity or dissimilarity overtones the
semantic similarity.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:25.090
Okay, then let’s see the solution to this
problem, as presented by the “Attention

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:29.100
is all you need” paper introducing the transformer.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:34.060
Their choice of positional embeddings is not
the easiest one and is best understood if

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:37.139
you have some knowledge of Fourier analysis.

00:04:37.139 --> 00:04:40.860
But let’s forget about that and explain
it simply:

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:46.990
You could think of the sequence as words coming
in one after the other.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:53.159
So the first idea is to take a non-periodic
function and number your sequence from 1 to

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:55.580
the total number of tokens you have.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:05:01.320
But with this recipe, we violate the second
requirement of positional embeddings, where

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:06.009
we said that shifts should be small, in other
words: bounded.

00:05:06.009 --> 00:05:12.400
Then let’s choose a function that is bounded
in the values it can take, like the sine or

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.539
cosine, periodically returning to values between
1 and -1.

00:05:17.539 --> 00:05:23.469
Sines and cosines have the upside that they
are defined up to infinity, so even with enormous

00:05:23.469 --> 00:05:30.949
sequence lengths, we would still have something
between -1 and 1 as elements for our positional

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:31.949
embeddings.

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:37.280
In comparison to a sigmoid, which is also
bounded, sines and cosines have a lot of variability

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.369
also for big numbers.

00:05:39.369 --> 00:05:42.449
Okay, so let’s choose a sine function to
model this.

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:48.199
But we see that with this periodic function,
the same outcome would repeat for different

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:49.199
positions.

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:53.389
Let’s suppose for a moment that we do not
want that and give our sine function such

00:05:53.389 --> 00:05:59.719
a low frequency, that even for our biggest
sequence length, the numbers would not repeat.

00:05:59.719 --> 00:06:04.930
With this, we almost have the same situation
as our linear function, because we numbered

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:10.819
our sequence in order, but this time, with
unique values between 0 and 1.

00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:15.349
This is still not optimal because we remember,
our positional embeddings push the input a

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:18.589
bit, to create “positional clubs”.

00:06:18.589 --> 00:06:23.159
But we also do not want to push by too much,
because otherwise distances between points

00:06:23.159 --> 00:06:27.149
become dominated by the position and not the
semantics.

00:06:27.149 --> 00:06:34.060
We also don’t want too little differences
between the first or the second token, because

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:40.930
in this case, the semantic signal would overtone
the positional embedding signal that now contains

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:45.699
values that differ very little between let’s
say the first or the second token.

00:06:45.699 --> 00:06:47.680
So what to do?

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:52.779
If one value in one dimension is too small
of a signal, let’s make our intent more

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:56.660
obvious by using all the other dimensions
too!

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:00.599
For the next dimension, we could use a cosine
which would look like this.

00:07:00.599 --> 00:07:05.979
Yet we still have the problem that embedding
values between one token and the next one,

00:07:05.979 --> 00:07:07.679
do not differ by much.

00:07:07.679 --> 00:07:13.529
But if we increase the frequency of the cosine,
the values differ more, because the slope

00:07:13.529 --> 00:07:14.529
has increased!

00:07:14.529 --> 00:07:20.199
Okay, so for this second dimension, let’s
use a cosine with increased frequency to create

00:07:20.199 --> 00:07:22.220
more differentiated values.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:27.990
The second and first dimension together are
uniquely differentiating tokens one from another,

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:32.719
but now also with values that differentiate
consecutive tokens more.

00:07:32.719 --> 00:07:35.729
And if we do this for every dimension, meaning
that we

00:07:35.729 --> 00:07:40.449
alternate between sines and cosines with increasing
frequency,

00:07:40.449 --> 00:07:46.099
we give enough information to ensure that
the transformer cannot miss this order of

00:07:46.099 --> 00:07:47.719
the sequence.

00:07:47.719 --> 00:07:54.240
So to recapitulate what we did: The last dimension
tells us broadly where the token is, like

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.370
is it more at the beginning or more toward
the end of the sequence.

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:03.559
But because the values are so continuous,
we do not have enough resolution to say if

00:08:03.559 --> 00:08:06.930
it is the first or the second token exactly.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:12.879
Then the second to last dimension has higher
frequency, thus higher resolution.

00:08:12.879 --> 00:08:17.709
Because we already know that we are at the
beginning of the sequence, we can pin down

00:08:17.709 --> 00:08:21.400
with more accuracy where we are exactly.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:27.089
The next dimensions pin down with even more
acuity where we are in the sequence.

00:08:27.089 --> 00:08:32.390
Well, this was the sine and cosine way of
encoding position.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:35.060
But this is only one way to do it.

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:39.180
This sine and cosine embedding seems to work
well on text,

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:42.240
but what if you have other types of data?

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.860
Like graphs.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:44.860
Or images!

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:50.050
Let us know, would you be interested in Relative
Position Representations and / or learned

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:51.440
positional embeddings?

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:56.410
If yes, write us in the comments and I could
convince Ms. Coffee Bean to cover this in

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:58.670
one of our very next videos!

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:04.370
Or let us know in the comments about any other
exotic or not-so exotic positional embeddings

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.470
you would like to know about.

